{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t---\n2\tname: local-model-oneshot-evaluation\n3\tdescription: \"User has the local DeepSeek model one-shot substantial features, then asks Claude to review — the review IS the capability measurement\"\n4\tmetadata: \n5\t  node_type: memory\n6\t  type: project\n7\t  originSessionId: f6bb2ded-a3cb-4342-afd7-bbadf34f59ee\n8\t  modified: 2026-08-04T02:45:00.306Z\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\tevintunador uses turnbridge features as a capability benchmark for the local\n12\tDeepSeek V4 Flash model (ds4-gateway): he asks it to one-shot a substantial\n13\ttask, opens a PR without smoke-testing it, and then asks Claude to review.\n14\tObserved 2026-08-03 on PR #3 (opencode target adapter): \"I'm looking to get an\n15\tidea of whether that complexity of task is doable for an open model.\"\n16\t\n17\t**Why:** the review's real purpose is two-sided — fix the PR *and* characterize\n18\twhere the open model's output breaks down. A verdict of \"looks fine\" without\n19\texecution is worthless to him; the interesting signal is which specific class\n20\tof thing the model got right versus wrong. On PR #3 the pattern was: format\n21\treverse-engineering from a real export was largely correct, but every defect\n22\tcame from validating only the happy path (tool-call payloads failed import\n23\toutright, and there were no tests at all).\n24\t\n25\t**How to apply:** when reviewing local-model work, actually execute it against\n26\tthe real CLI rather than reading it — the defects live in the branches the\n27\tmodel could not observe. Report the failure *pattern*, not just a defect list.\n28\tRelated: [[evintunador-workflow-preferences]], [[turnbridge-mvp-decisions]].\n29\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01DWsYqiRP8Zh8kSMDVCW8WL","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"173cfc66bb28f4c20e474f522f197eeef0c62